=document
=begin rakudoc
=TITLE Ringing in the changes
=SUBTITLE Using Raku++ to create website functionality

=config item :bullet<<\c[scroll]>>

=head Gifts this year

Two talented Raku community members - Andrey Shitov and Tokuhiro Matsuno - have developed two new
Raku compilers using Generative AI tools. Andrey's L«I<Raku++>| https://raku.online» compiles to C++ and Tahiro-san's L«I<mutsui>|https://tokuhirom.github.io/mutsu/»
compiles to Rust. Since there are now transpilers from C++ and Rust to WASM (a language that works inside a browser), both compiler stacks offer a way to write Website functionality in Raku.
I consider both Raku++ and mutsui to be amazing gifts to the Raku community this year, so thank you
to both Andrey and Tahiro-san.

Since I B<I<far>> prefer writing programs in I<Raku> than in I<Javascript>, I was excited to try 
one of the above to move functionality in the new documentation website to Raku.
=nested The new website
L<deployment | https://dev.docs.raku.org> is still being tested, but both Raku++ and mutsui can be invoked
in the website by clicking on the Raku icon on the right-hand side.

=para

For the purposes of this article, I can only use one of these projects, and there is a lot of
documentation to read. In addition, I have a bit more familiarity with C++ than Rust, but that should not matter, but it slightly tilted my decision to use Raku++ for this test. 

=head Greetings in other languages

If you take a look at the new Raku documentation website, you will see a drop-down to select the UX language.
The goal is to have Raku documentation in multiple languages, and for the UI language to be independent
of the content language (current multi-lingual sites change both the navigation and content together
which is a pain if you just want to check on some item in another language, but have to work out how to get to it in the other language).

Essentially all user-facing text is held in HTML spans with a key, and the web page is shipped with
a map from from the language / key to the text to be used.

What I want to explore is how much of the functionality currently implemented in Javascript can
be implemented in Raku, and whether there is a performance hit. Articles about WASM indicate that
Javascript will be faster for this sort of functionality and that other language implementations 
in WASM will be faster for calculation heavy functionality, so this particular example should not
have any performance benefit.

=head2 First: the final result

An illustration is better than a description, so here is the final result of
a stripped down example using both Javascript and Raku.

=begin Html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8">
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Greet the world</title>
    <script>
    var dictionary = { "en": { "LangName": "English", "HappyBirthday": "Happy Birthday", "Congratulations": "Congratulations", "HappyHolidays": "Happy holidays" }, "ua": { "LangName": "Українська", "HappyBirthday": "З днем ародження", "Congratulations": "Вітаю", "HappyHolidays": "Зі святами" }, "cy": { "LangName": "Cymraeg", "HappyBirthday": "Pen-blwydd hapus", "Congratulations": "Llongyfarchiadau", "HappyHolidays": "Gwyliau hapus" }, "ja": { "LangName": "日本語", "HappyBirthday": "お誕生日おめでとう", "Congratulations": "おめでとう", "HappyHolidays": "楽しい休日を" }, "nl": { "LangName": "Nederlands", "HappyBirthday": "Gefeliciteerd met je verjaardag", "Congratulations": "Gefeliciteerd", "HappyHolidays": "Fijne feestdagen" }, "epo": { "LangName": "Esperanto", "HappyBirthday": "Feliĉan naskiĝtagon", "Congratulations": "Gratulojn", "HappyHolidays": "Feliĉajn feriojn" } };
    </script>
    <script>
    function setUI( tSpans, newLang ) {
        tSpans.forEach( function (elem) {
            token = elem.getAttribute('data-UIToken');
            elem.innerHTML = dictionary[newLang][token];
        });
    };
    </script>
    <script>
        function makeUISelector( initial, selectorElem ) {
            var options = '';
            Object.keys(dictionary).forEach(function(key) {
                isSel = key == initial ? ' selected' : '';
                options = options +
                    '<option value="' + key + '"' + isSel + '>' + dictionary[key]['LangName'] + '</option>';
            });
            selectorElem.innerHTML = options;
        };
    </script>
    <script src="http://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/finanalyst/Advent2026_Articles/rFunctions.js"></script>
    <script>
        function rSetUI( tSpans, newLang ) {
            tSpans.forEach( function (elem) {
                token = elem.getAttribute('data-UIToken');
                elem.innerHTML = rDictionary(newLang,token);
            });
        };
    </script>
    <script>
    var defLang = 'en';
    var uiselector;
    var token_spans;
    var ruiselector;
    var rtoken_spans;
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
        uiselector = document.getElementById('Elucid8_ui-switch-contents');
        token_spans = document.querySelectorAll('.Elucid8-ui');
        makeUISelector( defLang, uiselector );
        setUI( token_spans, defLang );
        uiselector.addEventListener('change', (event) => {
            newLang = event.target.value;
            setUI( token_spans, newLang );
        });
        ruiselector = document.getElementById('rElucid8_ui-switch-contents');
        rtoken_spans = document.querySelectorAll('.r-Elucid8-ui');
        makeUISelector( defLang, ruiselector );
        rSetUI( rtoken_spans, defLang );
        ruiselector.addEventListener('change', (event) => {
            newLang = event.target.value;
            rSetUI( rtoken_spans, newLang );
        });
    });
    </script>
</head>
<body>
<table style="border: medium groove black;">
<tr><td>Greeting language</td>
<td>Javascript: <select id="Elucid8_ui-switch-contents"></select></td>
    <td>Raku: <select id="rElucid8_ui-switch-contents"></select></td>
</tr>
<tr><td>Greeting 1</td><td><span class="Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="HappyBirthday">Greeting 1</span></td><td><span class="r-Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="HappyBirthday">-</span></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Greeting 2</td><td><span class="Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="Congratulations">Greeting 2</span></td><td><span class="r-Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="Congratulations">-</span></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Greeting 3</td><td><span class="Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="HappyHolidays">Greeting 3</span></td><td><span class="r-Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="HappyHolidays">-</span></td></tr>
</table>
</body>

</html>


=end Html


=head2 The existing implementation

The following is an HTML file with a stripped down version of the Javascript-only program. 
If copied into a file and served, the Javascript selector will work, but the Raku one will not.
=begin numcode :lang<HTML>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8">
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Greet the world</title>
    <script>
    var dictionary = { "en": { "LangName": "English", "HappyBirthday": "Happy Birthday", "Congratulations": "Congratulations", "HappyHolidays": "Happy holidays" }, "ua": { "LangName": "Українська", "HappyBirthday": "З днем ародження", "Congratulations": "Вітаю", "HappyHolidays": "Зі святами" }, "cy": { "LangName": "Cymraeg", "HappyBirthday": "Pen-blwydd hapus", "Congratulations": "Llongyfarchiadau", "HappyHolidays": "Gwyliau hapus" }, "ja": { "LangName": "日本語", "HappyBirthday": "お誕生日おめでとう", "Congratulations": "おめでとう", "HappyHolidays": "楽しい休日を" }, "nl": { "LangName": "Nederlands", "HappyBirthday": "Gefeliciteerd met je verjaardag", "Congratulations": "Gefeliciteerd", "HappyHolidays": "Fijne feestdagen" }, "epo": { "LangName": "Esperanto", "HappyBirthday": "Feliĉan naskiĝtagon", "Congratulations": "Gratulojn", "HappyHolidays": "Feliĉajn feriojn" } };
    </script>
    <script>
    function setUI( tSpans, newLang ) {
        tSpans.forEach( function (elem) {
            token = elem.getAttribute('data-UIToken');
            elem.innerHTML = dictionary[newLang][token];
        });
    };
    </script>
    <script>
        function makeUISelector( initial, selectorElem ) {
            var options = '';
            Object.keys(dictionary).forEach(function(key) {
                isSel = key == initial ? ' selected' : '';
                options = options +
                    '<option value="' + key + '"' + isSel + '>' + dictionary[key]['LangName'] + '</option>';
            });
            selectorElem.innerHTML = options;
        };
    </script>
    <script>
    var defLang = 'en';
    var uiselector;
    var token_spans;
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
        uiselector = document.getElementById('Elucid8_ui-switch-contents');
        token_spans = document.querySelectorAll('.Elucid8-ui');
        makeUISelector( defLang, uiselector );
        setUI( token_spans, defLang );
        uiselector.addEventListener('change', (event) => {
            newLang = event.target.value;
            setUI( token_spans, newLang );
        });
    // placeholder for r-selector
    });
    </script>
</head>
<body>
<table style="border: medium groove black;">
<tr><td>Greeting language</td>
<td>Javascript: <select id="Elucid8_ui-switch-contents"></select></td>
    <td>Raku: <select id="rElucid8_ui-switch-contents"></select></td>
</tr>
<tr><td>Greeting 1</td><td><span class="Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="HappyBirthday">Greeting 1</span></td><td><span class="r-Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="HappyBirthday">-</span></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Greeting 2</td><td><span class="Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="Congratulations">Greeting 2</span></td><td><span class="r-Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="Congratulations">-</span></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Greeting 3</td><td><span class="Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="HappyHolidays">Greeting 3</span></td><td><span class="r-Elucid8-ui" data-uitoken="HappyHolidays">-</span></td></tr>
</table>
</body>

</html>
=end numcode
=head2 Main components

The Javascript program has been split into four components between C<script> tags:
=item a dictionary which contains the three greetings and language name keyed by language and token
=item code to generate the drop-down selection based on the languages in the dictionary
=item code to replace the text of spans with the selected language
=item code that runs when the DOM has settled, including a search to gather into an Array all the DOM elements containing spans that have tokens that need replacing.


=head Moving into Raku?

The first step is to move the dictionary to Raku (again this is nowhere near optimal given that the dictionary
is simply a native Javascript object).

Apart from the hash definition, the Raku program is trivial. Lets assume this is the content of
a text file called C<r-funtions.raku>:
=begin numcode
our %h =
    en => %(
        LangName => "English",
        HappyBirthday => "Happy Birthday",
        Congratulations => "Congratulations",
        HappyHolidays => "Happy holidays",
    ),
    ua => %(
        LangName => "Українська",
        HappyBirthday => "З днем ародження",
        Congratulations => "Вітаю",
        HappyHolidays => "Зі святами",
    ),
    cy => %(
        LangName => "Cymraeg",
        HappyBirthday => "Pen-blwydd hapus",
        Congratulations => "Llongyfarchiadau",
        HappyHolidays => "Gwyliau hapus",
    ),
    ja => %(
        LangName => "日本語",
        HappyBirthday => "お誕生日おめでとう",
        Congratulations => "おめでとう",
        HappyHolidays => "楽しい休日を",
    ),
    nl => %(
        LangName => "Nederlands",
        HappyBirthday => "Gefeliciteerd met je verjaardag",
        Congratulations => "Gefeliciteerd",
        HappyHolidays => "Fijne feestdagen",
    ),
    epo => %(
        LangName => "Esperanto",
        HappyBirthday => "Feliĉan naskiĝtagon",
        Congratulations => "Gratulojn",
        HappyHolidays => "Feliĉajn feriojn",
    ),
;
sub rDictionary( $lang, $token ) is export {
    %h{$lang}{$token}
}
=end numcode

After installing the C<raku++> compiler as described in L<Install raku++ | https://github.com/ash/rakupp/blob/main/docs/guide/INSTALL.md#debian-and-ubuntu-a-deb>,
 we compile this to a Javascript file called C<rFunctions.js> as follows
=for numcode :lang<text>
    raku++ -o rFunctions.js --js --standalone r-functions.raku
    
Notes:
=item The C<raku++> version must be > 5.11.0.
=item The rFunctions.js file needs to be served to the webpage, so the source used below is
from a github repo via a cdn (directly sourcing from github runs into a NoSniff error).
=item The options for C<raku++> are given in more detail in the project's dpcumentation, however
=item2 B<-o> is the output (compiled) file name; 
=item2 B<--js> indicates the compilation target; 
=item2 B<--standalone> indicates only one file is to be created; and 
=item2 the final option is the input file name


In order to link the Raku to the webpage, we add two scripts into the document C<head> section, namely
=begin numcode :lang<HTML>
<script src="http://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/finanalyst/Advent2026_Articles/rFunctions.js"></script>
<script>
    function rSetUI( tSpans, newLang ) {
        tSpans.forEach( function (elem) {
            token = elem.getAttribute('data-UIToken');
            elem.innerHTML = rDictionary(newLang,token);
        });
    };
</script>
=end numcode
Since the Javascript file has to be served, and the context of this blog is a bit complicated,
I have loaded the file from a public github repo, which contains the RakuDoc source of this article
and the raku programs.

In addition, we add the following in place of the comment C<// placeholder for Raku call>
=begin numcode :lang<Javascript> :numalias<RSEL>
ruiselector = document.getElementById('rElucid8_ui-switch-contents');
rtoken_spans = document.querySelectorAll('.r-Elucid8-ui');
makeUISelector( defLang, ruiselector );
rSetUI( rtoken_spans, defLang );
ruiselector.addEventListener('change', (event) => {
    newLang = event.target.value;
    rSetUI( rtoken_spans, newLang );
});
=end numcode

=head2 Populating DOM elements from within Raku

In order to introspect the DOM of the HTML file, and to modify it, C<raku++> has the C<use js>
pragma. The pragma creates a new C<JS> object, which can then be treated as I<the gloabal This>.

We add the following function to our C<r-functions.raku> file:
=begin numcode
use js;
sub changeLang( $new-lang, $tag-class) is export {
	my $doc = JS.document;
	my @targets = $doc.querySelectorAll( $tag-class );
	for @targets {
		my $token = .getAttribute('data-UIToken');
		.innerHTML = %h{ $new-lang}{$token};
	}
}
=end numcode

Then we replace the snippet in I<A<RSEL>> with 
=begin numcode :lang<Javascript>
ruiselector = document.getElementById('rElucid8_ui-switch-contents');
makeUISelector( defLang, ruiselector );
changeLang( defLang, '.r-Elucid8-ui');
rSetUI( rtoken_spans, defLang );
ruiselector.addEventListener('change', (event) => {
    newLang = event.target.value;
    changeLang( newLang, '.r-Elucid8-ui');
});
=end numcode

=head2 Moving the selector creation to Raku

Now we take the final step by populating the language selector from the keys of the 
hash, and attach a listener to the selector all inside a Raku function.


=end rakudoc